To retrieve the interfaces (and other schema information) from a GraphQL endpoint, you use a feature built into GraphQL called **Introspection**. Introspection allows you to query the GraphQL server for information about its own schema.

Here is how you can retrieve the interfaces, the fields they contain, and the object types that implement them.

### 1. The Introspection Query
You can send the following GraphQL query to the endpoint. It queries the `__schema` and asks for all types, but returns the `kind`, `name`, `fields`, and `possibleTypes` (the objects that implement the interface).

```graphql
query GetInterfaces {
  __schema {
    types {
      kind
      name
      description
      # What fields the interface requires
      fields {
        name
        description
        type {
          name
          kind
        }
      }
      # Which object types implement this interface
      possibleTypes {
        name
        kind
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Because GraphQL doesn't allow you to filter by `kind` directly inside the query, this will return *all* types. You will need to look through the JSON response and filter for objects where `"kind": "INTERFACE"`.

### 2. How to execute the query

**Via cURL (Command Line):**
You can send this query using a standard HTTP POST request.
```bash
curl -X POST \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"query": "query { __schema { types { kind name fields { name } possibleTypes { name } } } }"}' \
  https://your-graphql-endpoint.com/graphql
```

**Via JavaScript (Fetch API):**
```javascript
const query = `
  query {
    __schema {
      types {
        kind
        name
      }
    }
  }
`;

fetch('https://your-graphql-endpoint.com/graphql', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ query }),
})
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => {
    // Filter the results to only show interfaces
    const interfaces = data.data.__schema.types.filter(t => t.kind === 'INTERFACE');
    console.log(interfaces);
  });
```

### 3. Using GraphQL Tools (The easiest way)
If you don't want to write raw introspection queries and filter JSON, you can use UI tools that automatically run introspection and format it nicely:

*   **GraphiQL / Apollo Studio / GraphQL Playground:** If the endpoint has a web interface enabled, just open it in your browser. Look for the "Docs" or "Schema" tab on the right side. You can search for interfaces there.
*   **Postman:** If you import a GraphQL endpoint into Postman, it can automatically fetch the schema and provide auto-complete and documentation views.
*   **CLI Tools:** You can use an NPM package like [`get-graphql-schema`](https://github.com/prisma-labs/get-graphql-schema). 
    ```bash
    npx get-graphql-schema https://your-graphql-endpoint.com/graphql > schema.graphql
    ```
    Once downloaded, you can open `schema.graphql` in a text editor and search for the keyword `interface`.

### ⚠️ Important Note: Introspection in Production
Many public or production GraphQL APIs **disable introspection** for security reasons (to prevent attackers from easily mapping out the API). If you run the query above and get an error saying `GraphQL introspection is not allowed`, it means the server has explicitly turned this feature off, and you will not be able to retrieve the interfaces without the API provider's official documentation.

